    "name": "From SELECT to Risk Measures",
    "cells": [
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "# From SELECT to Risk Measures — A Classroom Lab on Relative vs. Absolute Effect\n\n> **⚠ DATA & SCOPE NOTICE (read first)**\n> All patient-level records in this notebook are **synthetic**: identifiers are **deterministic, fictional, and guaranteed unique**, while outcome assignment and row order are randomized within each arm (fixed seed). **No attempt is made to reconstruct actual trial participants.** Aggregate event counts and denominators are taken only from the published SELECT trial report (Lincoff et al., *NEJM* 2023; DOI 10.1056/NEJMoa2307563). This notebook is an **educational exercise, not medical advice**.\n\n**Learning goals.** After working through this lab you should be able to:\n1. Compute crude **event proportions**, **risk ratio**, **risk difference**, **absolute risk reduction (ARR)**, and an **approximate NNT** from a 2×2 table.\n2. Construct an approximate 95% CI for a crude risk ratio (Katz log method).\n3. Explain why a **crude risk ratio from aggregate counts is not the same as a published hazard ratio**.\n4. Critically interpret **relative vs. absolute** treatment effects and discuss limitations (composite endpoints, follow-up duration, generalizability, missing results).\n\n**Published SELECT aggregates used in this lab:**\n- Semaglutide: 569 primary composite events among 8,803 participants.\n- Placebo: 701 primary composite events among 8,801 participants.\n- Published time-to-first-event hazard ratio: 0.80 (95% CI 0.72–0.90).\n- Primary composite: cardiovascular death, nonfatal myocardial infarction, or nonfatal stroke.\n- Mean follow-up: 39.8 months; individual follow-up durations varied.\n- Trial population: adults aged 45 years or older with BMI at least 27 and established cardiovascular disease, without diabetes.\n\n**Run order and requirements.** Use the Python kernel. Run `synthetic-data`, then `analysis-solution`, then `final-summary`. The code uses only Python's standard library; it does not install packages or fetch patient data. The shuffled fictional rows preserve the reported counts, so changing their random seed changes neither the counts nor these effect estimates.\n\n**Scope of the calculation.** Events divided by randomized participants are crude event proportions over the observed follow-up. They are not censoring-adjusted cumulative risks at a common time point. Consequently, 1/ARR here is a classroom NNT approximation, not a published or fixed-horizon survival-analysis estimate.\n\n**Primary references:** [Lincoff et al., SELECT report, NEJM 2023](https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMoa2307563); [Altman and Andersen, time-specific NNT, BMJ 1999](https://www.bmj.com/content/319/7223/1492).",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "title-goals"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## Student Questions — compute these from the synthetic data\n\nUsing the dataset generated in the next cell, answer the following for each group, then compute the between-group measures:\n\n1. **Crude event proportion** for the primary composite outcome in the semaglutide group and in the placebo group.\n2. **Crude risk ratio** (semaglutide vs. placebo).\n3. **Risk difference** (semaglutide − placebo), in percentage points.\n4. **Absolute risk reduction (ARR)**, in percentage points.\n5. **Crude approximate NNT** — both 1/ARR (unrounded) and the conventional rounded-up integer. State why these event counts do not determine a censoring-adjusted NNT at a fixed time.\n\n*Extension:* derive an approximate 95% confidence interval for the crude risk ratio from the 2×2 counts, and state which method you used.\n\nTry these by hand or in code before looking at the solution cell.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "student-questions"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# Synthetic patient-level dataset for the SELECT trial (Lincoff et al., NEJM 2023, DOI 10.1056/NEJMoa2307563)\n# All rows are FICTIONAL/SYNTHETIC: IDs are deterministic and guaranteed unique;\n# outcome assignment and row order are randomized within each arm with a fixed seed.\n# The exact group denominators and event counts are preserved.\n# NOT medical advice.\n\nimport random\nrng = random.Random(42)\n\nn_sema, n_placebo = 8803, 8801\ne_sema, e_placebo = 569, 701\n\ndef make_group(n, events, label):\n    # Sequential zero-padded IDs guarantee uniqueness (no collisions by construction)\n    ids = [f\"{label}-{i:06d}\" for i in range(1, n + 1)]\n    outcomes = [1] * events + [0] * (n - events)\n    rng.shuffle(outcomes)  # randomize outcome assignment within group\n    rows = [{\"id\": pid, \"treatment\": label, \"primary_outcome\": o}\n            for pid, o in zip(ids, outcomes)]\n    rng.shuffle(rows)  # randomize row order within group\n    return rows\n\ndata = make_group(n_sema, e_sema, \"semaglutide\") + make_group(n_placebo, e_placebo, \"placebo\")\nrng.shuffle(data)\n\nprint(f\"Total synthetic rows: {len(data)} (exact denominators preserved)\")\nprint(f\"Unique IDs: {len({r['id'] for r in data})}\")\nprint(\"\\nSample of first 10 rows:\")\nfor r in data[:10]:\n    print(f\"  {r['id']:>18} | {r['treatment']:>12} | primary_outcome = {r['primary_outcome']}\")\n\n# validation\nsema = [r for r in data if r[\"treatment\"] == \"semaglutide\"]\nplac = [r for r in data if r[\"treatment\"] == \"placebo\"]\nassert len(sema) == n_sema and len(plac) == n_placebo\nassert sum(r[\"primary_outcome\"] for r in sema) == e_sema\nassert sum(r[\"primary_outcome\"] for r in plac) == e_placebo\nassert len({r[\"id\"] for r in data}) == len(data), \"duplicate synthetic IDs detected\"\nprint(\"\\nAssertions passed: exact group sizes, event counts, and unique IDs.\")",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "synthetic-data"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# Analysis cell — derives all answers from `data`, no hard-coded results.\n# These are crude event proportions, not censoring-adjusted fixed-time risks.\nimport math\n\nsema = [r for r in data if r[\"treatment\"] == \"semaglutide\"]\nplac = [r for r in data if r[\"treatment\"] == \"placebo\"]\n\nn1, e1 = len(sema), sum(r[\"primary_outcome\"] for r in sema)\nn0, e0 = len(plac), sum(r[\"primary_outcome\"] for r in plac)\n\nassert n1 == 8803 and n0 == 8801\nassert e1 == 569 and e0 == 701\n\nrisk_sema   = e1 / n1\nrisk_placebo = e0 / n0\nrr  = risk_sema / risk_placebo\nrd  = risk_sema - risk_placebo          # risk difference (semaglutide - placebo)\narr = risk_placebo - risk_sema          # absolute risk reduction\nnnt_approx = 1 / arr\nnnt_conv   = math.ceil(nnt_approx)\n\n# Approximate 95% CI for crude RR via the Katz log method on the 2x2 counts\nse_log_rr = math.sqrt(1/e1 - 1/n1 + 1/e0 - 1/n0)\nrr_lo = math.exp(math.log(rr) - 1.96 * se_log_rr)\nrr_hi = math.exp(math.log(rr) + 1.96 * se_log_rr)\n\nprint(f\"Crude event proportion (semaglutide) = {risk_sema:.4%}\")\nprint(f\"Crude event proportion (placebo)     = {risk_placebo:.4%}\")\nprint(f\"Crude event-proportion ratio (RR) = {rr:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Risk difference (sema - placebo) = {rd*100:+.3f} percentage points\")\nprint(f\"Absolute risk reduction       = {arr*100:.3f} percentage points\")\nprint(f\"1/ARR (crude approximate NNT)       = {nnt_approx:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"Conventional rounded-up NNT   = {nnt_conv}\")\nprint(f\"\\nApproximate 95% CI for crude RR (Katz log method on 2x2 counts):\")\nprint(f\"  RR = {rr:.4f} (95% CI {rr_lo:.4f} – {rr_hi:.4f})\")\nprint(\"NNT is a crude classroom approximation from aggregate counts,\")\nprint(\"not a censoring-adjusted estimate at a common time horizon.\")",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "analysis-solution"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## Why the crude risk ratio is NOT the published hazard ratio\n\nThe SELECT publication reports a **hazard ratio of 0.80 (95% CI 0.72–0.90)** from a time-to-first-event analysis. That quantity is fundamentally different from the **crude event-proportion ratio (≈0.81)** computed in this lab:\n\n- A **hazard ratio** comes from a Cox-type model that uses **the timing of each event and the censoring pattern** (when participants' follow-up ends) — it compares instantaneous event *rates* over the whole follow-up period.\n- The **crude risk ratio** here uses only the final 2×2 tally (events / participants per arm). It ignores *when* events happened and *when* follow-up ended.\n- The published SELECT facts given to this lab contain **no event times and no censoring information**, so a hazard ratio (or its confidence interval) **cannot be reconstructed** from these counts. **We deliberately do not invent event times** — any such reconstruction would be fabrication.\n\nThe crude RR (0.8115) and published HR (0.80) are numerically close here, but they measure different quantities and need not agree. These counts cannot explain or reconstruct the published hazard ratio, especially when follow-up, censoring, competing risks, or the hazard over time matter.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "hr-explanation"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## Interpretation: Relative vs. Absolute Effects\n\nThe crude event-proportion ratio of **0.81** means the observed primary-composite event proportion was about 19% lower in the semaglutide arm than in the placebo arm. This descriptive ratio is not the published time-to-event hazard ratio.\n\nThe **crude absolute risk reduction (ARR) of ~1.5 percentage points** helps show the scale of the difference, alongside its uncertainty, harms, costs, and patient preferences:\n\n- Crude placebo event proportion ≈ 7.97% → semaglutide event proportion ≈ 6.46%\n- The reciprocal of that difference (≈1.5 in 100 participants) is a **crude approximate NNT of ~67**. This is an arithmetic summary of the supplied event proportions. SELECT's mean follow-up was 39.8 months, but follow-up varied: these counts do not give a censoring-adjusted NNT at 39.8 months or any other fixed time.\n\n**Key point:** a large relative reduction (19%) can coexist with a modest absolute benefit (1.5 points) when the baseline event rate is relatively low. Always report both measures — relative measures can exaggerate perceived benefit, while absolute measures (ARR, NNT) make the real-world scale of benefit clearer.",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "interpretation"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## Discussion Questions\n\n1. **NNT depends on follow-up and endpoint definition.** The crude approximation of ~67 describes the supplied trial counts, population, and composite endpoint; it is not a fixed-time survival-analysis estimate. How would a longer or shorter follow-up, or a narrower endpoint (e.g., only cardiovascular death), change the ARR and NNT? Why must NNT always be reported *with* its time horizon and endpoint?\n\n2. **Composite endpoints.** The primary outcome pools cardiovascular death, nonfatal MI, and nonfatal stroke. What are the advantages (statistical power, clinically coherent outcome) and risks (components of unequal severity or frequency driving the result) of composite endpoints?\n\n3. **Cause-specific vs. all-cause mortality.** A treatment can reduce cause-specific cardiovascular death without changing all-cause mortality (e.g., if deaths from other causes offset the reduction, or if the specific cause is a small share of all deaths). Why is this distinction important when communicating benefit, and why can neither be assessed from the 2×2 counts supplied here? *Note: this lab supplies no mortality numbers, and none should be inferred.*\n\n4. **Safety and discontinuation.** Benefit estimates from the primary endpoint do not by themselves establish net benefit. What safety, tolerability, and discontinuation evidence would you want before weighing benefit against harm? (This lab contains **no adverse-event data**, and none should be invented.)\n\n5. **Generalizability.** SELECT enrolled adults with overweight/obesity and established cardiovascular disease, without diabetes. To whom do these risk measures apply — and to whom might they not? How do baseline risk differences in other populations change ARR and NNT even if the relative effect is similar?\n\n6. **Absence of evidence ≠ evidence of absence.** If a reported secondary endpoint or safety outcome is *missing*, *nonsignificant*, or numerically unfavorable but with a wide confidence interval, why is that **not proof of hidden harm** (nor of hidden benefit)? What would you need — point estimates with CIs, adequate power, and pre-specified analyses — to make a defensible claim either way?",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "discussion-questions"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# Final summary — consolidates all validated results for quick reference\nprint(\"=\" * 60)\nprint(\"SELECT AGGREGATES (SYNTHETIC ROWS) — CLASSROOM SUMMARY\")\nprint(\"=\" * 60)\nprint(f\"Denominators preserved: semaglutide n={len(sema)}, placebo n={len(plac)}\")\nprint(f\"Event counts preserved: semaglutide e={e1}, placebo e={e0}\")\nprint(f\"Crude proportion, semaglutide: {risk_sema:.4%}\")\nprint(f\"Crude proportion, placebo: {risk_placebo:.4%}\")\nprint(f\"Crude risk ratio  : {rr:.4f}  (approx 95% CI {rr_lo:.4f}–{rr_hi:.4f}, Katz log method)\")\nprint(f\"Risk difference   : {rd*100:+.3f} pp\")\nprint(f\"ARR               : {arr*100:.3f} pp\")\nprint(f\"NNT (crude approx): {nnt_approx:.2f}\")\nprint(f\"NNT (conventional): {nnt_conv}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 60)\nprint(\"LIMITATIONS\")\nprint(\"- Patient rows, row order, and IDs are synthetic; counts are published aggregates.\")\nprint(\"- Crude RR ≠ published HR (0.80, 95% CI 0.72–0.90): HR uses time-to-event\")\nprint(\"  and censoring info we cannot reconstruct from aggregate counts alone.\")\nprint(\"- Mean follow-up was 39.8 months, with varying individual follow-up.\")\nprint(\"- The NNT is crude, not censoring-adjusted at a fixed time.\")\nprint(\"- No safety, subgroup, or secondary-endpoint data included.\")\nprint(\"- Educational exercise only; not medical advice.\")",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "final-summary"
      }
    ]
  }
}
